{:edb/db-before-t 1819 :edb/db-after-t 1820 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535132 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:42:04.777Z" 13194139535132 true] [17592186151872 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4$." 13194139535132 false] [17592186151872 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4.$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186151872 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = t\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{t^{3}}{t^{2}} = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{y^{3}}{x^{2}}ds &= ∫_{0}^{4}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}t \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{4}tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}t^{2} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(4^{2} - 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 false] [17592186151872 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186151873 1100 "Consider the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\n where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9).$\nWrite the line integral in the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186151873 1100 "Consider the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9)$.\nWrite the line integral in the form $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t$." 13194139535132 false] [17592186151873 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x)$. Therefore, by setting $x=t$, we have\n\n$$\ny = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{3}}{3})j \\\\\n&= i + t^{2}j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (t^{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= ((\\frac{t^{3}}{3})^{2})/(t) = \\frac{1}{9}t^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)ds &= ∫_{0}^{3}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}t^{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + t^{4}} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}∫_{0}^{3}t^{5}\\sqrt{1 + t^{4}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 false] [17592186151873 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3,\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})j \\\\  & =i+{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})}^{2}}{t}=\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186152081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332872 13194139535132 true] [17592186152081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332879 13194139535132 true] [17592186152081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332886 13194139535132 true] [17592186152081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332907 13194139535132 true] [17592186152082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332851 13194139535132 true] [17592186152082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332858 13194139535132 true] [17592186152082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332865 13194139535132 true] [17592186152082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332893 13194139535132 true] [17592186152082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332900 13194139535132 true] [17592186152666 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535132 true] [17592186152666 1100 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2y^{2}$ along the curve $y = e^{x}$, $0 \\le x \\le \\ln 3$." 13194139535132 false] [17592186152666 1100 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186152666 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332852 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1089 "q-148205" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1113 #uuid "d45e7151-1e23-5fc3-b354-b72fb9448af9" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332851 1145 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2{e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+2{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332853 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332854 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332855 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332856 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332857 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1041 "selection" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332854 13194139535132 true] [17592186332852 1110 #uuid "36f1a1e4-0759-599c-93ca-97425a07f999" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332853 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535132 true] [17592186332853 1111 #uuid "c62ed926-d2c6-53f5-aef8-3ea86c727387" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332854 1034 "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+4{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535132 true] [17592186332854 1111 #uuid "002f2f6a-cece-5aee-a1b8-db89629437bb" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332855 1034 "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535132 true] [17592186332855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535132 true] [17592186332855 1111 #uuid "